<h2>The background</h2>

<p>The petitioner, national president of a students' association, posted an invitation to a gathering at Mandi House on 16 October 2026 amid protests concerning electoral roll revision. She was detained that day and released the next. On 17 October, a Special Executive Magistrate issued a notice under Sections 126/130 BNSS, founded on a police kalandara citing repeated protests despite a prohibitory order under Section 163 BNSS (formerly Section 144 CrPC), an unauthorised demonstration call for Connaught Place, three general diary entries and six FIRs. The notice asked why she should not execute a personal bond of Rs 10,000 with one surety under Section 135(3) for keeping the peace.</p>

<h2>The rival cases and the holding</h2>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>The petitioner</h3><p>The notice mechanically reproduced statutory language; the SEM formed no independent opinion and acted on senior officers' directions; the information was hearsay; Mandi House was outside the Section 163 order; a peaceful protest cannot breach the peace.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>The State</h3><p>The SEM received the kalandara and supporting material, heard the investigating officer and formed a subjective opinion; the writ court checks only whether material existed; the prohibitory order covered Mandi House; remedies, including discharge under Section 137, remain open.</p></div>
</div>

<p>The court dismissed the writ. It found the notice met the requirements of a show-cause notice under Chapter IX; the SEM had material — the kalandara, GD entries and FIRs — and the court "cannot sit in the arm-chair" of the Magistrate to re-test his subjective satisfaction. The Section 163 order of 24 August 2026 was held to cover Mandi House. On the recurrence argument, the court said even one incident is sufficient to initiate proceedings if the circumstances suggest a likelihood of further breaches.</p>

<h2>The safeguards the judgment locks in</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Disclosure</h3><p>The State must supply all material relied upon before the enquiry — the person cannot be made to answer an undisclosed case.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Natural justice</h3><p>The SEM must ensure a fair hearing before any bond order is made.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Interim bonds restrained</h3><p>Before any interim bond under Section 135(3), the SEM must consider Section 135 and the Madhu Limaye safeguards; whether to insist on a bond at the first instance is for his own, independently applied mind.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Scope confined</h3><p>The observations are limited to Article 226 interference at the show-cause stage; the merits remain open in the enquiry and in discharge under Section 137.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Why this matters beyond protest cases</h2>

<p>Preventive proceedings touch far more people than public demonstrations: neighbourhood disputes, apprehended land-grab violence and recurring local quarrels are routinely routed through Executive Magistrates in Delhi. The CrPC-to-BNSS transition renumbered the chapter — Section 107 became 126, Section 111 became 130, Section 116(3) became 135(3), Section 118 discharge became 137 — without changing its essential character, and this judgment confirms that the half-century of safeguards built around the old chapter, beginning with Madhu Limaye, travels into the new code. A person served with such a notice should demand the complete material, test whether the Magistrate's order under Section 130 states the substance of the information, and remember that the proceeding ends either in a time-bound bond or in discharge — it is not a criminal trial, and an unjustified interim bond demand is itself challengeable.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article summarises a Delhi High Court judgment on preventive proceedings under the BNSS for general information. Preventive action cases turn on the material before the Executive Magistrate in each case; nothing here is legal advice.</p></div>
